Round 1 review on 6ac09d0f. Fix these in ONE new commit on top (no amend, no push), same worktree /Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/.worktrees/regression-batch2, same verification (full suite -n auto --cov 100%, new tests 3x, prek), proofs only in the /private/tmp scratch copy restored in finally. Still TEST-ONLY; stop and report if a fix exposes a production bug.

1. #405 (test_binary_sensor.py ~2384): "no writes while offline" is vacuous — a disconnected lock raises in _check_reachable before async_set_usercode, so service_calls/run_times can never record a retry. Observe the spam #405 was about: count the attempts that reach the availability probe while offline (you added `probe` faults; add a recording or counting mode if needed, test-only) and assert the attempts follow the backoff (exact count over the offline window if stable). Re-prove: it must fail with the offline gate/backoff reverted so sync retries every tick.
2. PR #962 (test_sync.py ~359): service_calls["set_usercode"] only records writes that change the code; repeated NO_CHANGE writes of the same PIN are invisible, so a regression that rewrites the write-only slot every tick passes. Count every set that reaches the lock (run_times, or record no-op sets the way batch 0 records no-op clears — test-only) and assert exactly one set of the new PIN over the quiet window. Also make the "set once" step observable (the first tick_until took 0 ticks). Re-prove with a mutation that rewrites the unreadable slot every tick.
3. Minimum tick budgets — give each real margin (or a condition-based wait sized with headroom), and record the measured taken/max in your report:
   - test_init.py ~1367 'slot 1 suspended again' (5 of 5);
   - test_base.py ~2963 #1526/#1527 'slot 1 in sync once the lock is set up' (3 of 3);
   - test_base.py ~2742 `_async_set_up_slots` fixed max_ticks=3 regardless of slot count (3 of 3 with 4 slots) — scale with the slot count;
   - test_binary_sensor.py ~658 `_TICKS_TO_THE_BACKOFF_PROBE` (59 of 65; one more failure doubles the backoff) — derive it from the backoff the lock is actually in or give a full extra backoff step of headroom;
   - test_binary_sensor.py ~1486 #955 first tick_until (3 of 3);
   - test_sync.py ~1665 and the #1523 / #1527-probe tests: advance the deadline under test plus one tick rather than exactly the deadline (keep the 30 s vs 600 s distinction meaningful: e.g. assert NOT done just before the deadline if that's stable, done one tick after).
4. tests/common.py ~1217: a `setup`/`probe` fault given a slot silently matches nothing. Reject a slot for setup/probe in __post_init__ like ignore/refuse on reads; add a harness test.
5. #955 (test_binary_sensor.py ~1486): no code change needed beyond item 3, but make the docstring say what it guards: the conversion of text slot keys in storage (slot_assignment.py) — the int() casts in sync.py/slot_coordinator.py are unreachable behind it (recorded in the catalog as dead casts).
6. #1050 (test_binary_sensor.py ~1263): no change needed — the three breaker resets are redundant, so only removing all three fails; the test guards the outcome. Mention that in its docstring in one line so nobody "fixes" the test later.

Commit message: `test: count what reaches an offline lock; give tick budgets headroom` + short body + trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Report: per item the change, re-proof results, measured taken/max for each budget, suite/coverage/prek, SHA.

content.text:
Batch 3a has started on `test/regression-batch3`, branched from main. A subagent is adding tests that go only through what Home Assistant exposes for 15 lifecycle and shared-lock bugs:

- **Startup and connection:** the #225 cluster (no entities while the lock's integration isn't loaded), the #247 startup hang, codes written before the first read, #1048 and #1268 (entities stuck unavailable, or "recovered" on empty data).
- **Shared locks:**
  - an unload traceback (#251);
  - a concurrent-startup race (#865);
  - duplicate events from orphaned listeners (#1476);
  - a lock's stored codes deleted while another entry still uses it (#1482);
  - removing one lock wiping another lock's codes (#1538). That one was never reproduced, so its test is cheap insurance.
- **Repairs and degradation:** the "lock offline" repair flashing at startup (#1257), slow recovery after a lock-only outage (#1289), a failed setup's stale config leaking into the options form (#1632), and a lock that fails its capability check vanishing from the entry (#1344).
- **Services after the last unload (#928).** No test covers it today.

Failures are triggered the way they really happen: the lock's integration is down, or the provider setup or read fails. No LCM internals are patched. The same rules as batches 1 and 2 apply. Each test has to fail when its fix is reverted, and if one turns up a real bug, the subagent reports it rather than fixing it.

It's also checking the open lead from the lock-handle review: after a failed setup, LCM may keep reacting to the lock's integration changing state. If that reproduces, it will rerun the check on the commit before #1629 to see whether #1629 introduced it.

Batch 3b, reworking the lock-handle state machine against these surfaces, starts once 3a is in.

